3. The brief facts of the present case are that on 27.05.2005, the Food Inspector Sh. Gian Chand and Sh. J.P. Bhardwaj, Field Assistant under the supervision of Local Health Authority/SDM Sh.
R.K.Sharma, reached at the premises of appellant i.e. M/s Shivam Store, Vashishth Market, Labour Chownk-25 Foota Road, Burari, Delhi-84, where accused Vendor cum Proprietor was found conducting business of various food articles including Arhar Dal which were lying stored for sale for human consumption ;
- that FI disclosed his identity and expressed his intention to purchase a sample of commodity from the vendor which was lying in an open gunny bag bearing no label declaration, to which he agreed ;
- that thereafter as per the prescribed procedure under the PFA Act and Rules after giving notice to the vendor/appellant and offer towards the sale price of sample to which he agreed and accepted the sale consideration, sample was then lifted ;
CA no. 8699/16 Datta Ram Bharti Vs. Delhi Admn/Food Inspector pages 2 of 13 - that each sample was separately packed, fastened, marked, sealed and necessary documents including Notice as per Form-VI in the presence of appellant/accused alongwith Panchnama etc was prepared at the spot ; the price of the sample commodity was paid to the vendor ;
- that thereafter one counterpart of sample was sent to Public Analyst (PA) in intact condition and other two counterparts were deposited with SDM/LHA ;
- that vide report dated 17.06.2005, PA found the sample to be adulterated on the ground that it was coloured with synthetic colouring matter "Tartrazine" ;
- that upon receipt of report, SDM/LHA directed investigation which was carried out by Food Inspector and after completion of investigation sanction under Section 20 of the PFA Act was obtained from the Director PFA.
